WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.281 --> 00:00:02.472
(light music)
- We take this bracket out,

00:00:02.472 --> 00:00:03.680
get a better...

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.113
The microphone on this side.
- Yeah.

00:00:05.113 --> 00:00:08.399
- Almost 300 million of
these were sold in 2021

00:00:08.399 --> 00:00:10.950
and AirPods are by far the most popular.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.150
They made up about a third

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.610
of all earbuds shipped
around the world last year.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.770
But unlike an iPhone or a Mac,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.320
if one of these starts to have an issue,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.760
Apple might replace it,

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.730
but it won't repair them
or help you do it yourself.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:24.750
Why is that?

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.050
To find out, we're gonna
take a look inside an AirPod

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.410
and ask technicians how
repairable they actually are.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.510
Yeah, so tell me a little bit
about what you do at iFixit.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.960
- Our job at iFixit is to
assess the repairability

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.120
of electronic and consumer devices.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.430
Anything that comes
across our teardown table,

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.950
we break apart to see
how repairable it is.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.810
- How hard is it to get into these things?

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.930
- If you want to take this
apart, non-destructively,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.550
to replace the batteries,

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.890
as far as I know, that's impossible.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.200
- So we're starting by melting the glue.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.033
I'm using this

00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:58.020
to melt the glue?
- Yep.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.120
- God, this feels so wrong.
(heat gun whirs)

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.610
- Oh, it's separating. There you go.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:03.690
Yeah. Let's take a break here.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:04.523
- Oh no. Okay.

00:01:04.523 --> 00:01:05.520
- One of the reasons they use glue

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.380
is because, , it's a very effective way

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.980
of preventing sweat and
moisture from getting inside.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.010
The question is, is it worth the cost?

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:14.971
- We can replace the battery now.

00:01:14.971 --> 00:01:17.904
(both chuckle)

00:01:17.904 --> 00:01:19.050
- The AirPod's not gonna work anymore,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.580
but you could replace the battery.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.413
Okay.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:23.243
There you go.
- I got it.

00:01:23.243 --> 00:01:24.076
- You got it.
- Boom.

00:01:24.076 --> 00:01:25.410
- Nice. All right.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.690
- I think our best bet is
just to use the sonic knife

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:28.740
and go right through.

00:01:29.783 --> 00:01:31.170
(knife squeaking)

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.778
- Okay. That definitely went through.

00:01:33.778 --> 00:01:35.280
Oh, okay. We got it.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.110
Do you think Apple could
put this thing together?

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.550
- They won't service these things.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.020
I would love to be proven wrong.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.107
I would love for Apple to say,

00:01:41.107 --> 00:01:43.770
"Actually, yeah we can get
into these and fix these."

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.600
- [Host] So what exactly
happens to AirPods

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.040
after they have issues?

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.590
- Well, Apple says it
will replace your AirPod

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:50.790
and send the previous one

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.880
to a best-in-class recycler
for responsible handling.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.550
Apple doesn't provide
repair manuals for AirPods

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.830
the way it does for some iPhones and Macs.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.570
That makes it that much more difficult

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.910
for third-party technicians
to make repairs.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.290
And like we saw earlier,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.630
since these are so difficult to get into,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.690
repairing them probably isn't
the most practical option.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:10.590
When it comes to repairability,

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.960
iFixit rated the original AirPods Pro

00:02:15.087 --> 00:02:16.410
a definitive zero out of 10

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.630
and says it's one of the most unrepairable

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.010
among its competitors.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.600
So why does that matter?

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.060
Well, Apple shipped more
than 90 million earbuds

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.800
in 2022 alone.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.200
Within a couple of years,
a lot of those AirPods

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.990
will end up being recycled
or tossed in the trash

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.300
because of this part right here.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.760
Experts say the batteries
used in Bluetooth earbuds

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.380
only last about 18 months

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.970
before they start to have problems.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.380
Let's take a look at this graph.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.480
While not focused specifically on AirPods,

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.660
it generally shows how
lithium ion batteries age.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.110
The x-axis shows how many
times the battery is cycled

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.910
or charged and discharged.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.850
The y-axis shows how much
the battery can store

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.620
compared to its initial max.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.460
Notice that around 350 cycles,
things really drop off.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.860
The lead researcher told
me that drop in the graph

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.500
is the reason your device
can work fine one day

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.500
and have performance issues
and less battery life the next.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.570
Apple acknowledges that
rechargeable batteries

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.160
have a limited lifespan.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:12.600
It says how long they last

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.580
depends on how often you charge them.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.170
Eventually, performance issues

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.750
might send you back to the
Apple store for a new pair

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.120
or those old earbuds
may end up in a drawer,

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.040
the garbage or the recycling bin.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.320
So can AirPods be repaired?

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.153
- Yes.

00:03:26.153 --> 00:03:27.540
If you want, you can try at home,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.920
although I don't recommend.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.300
It's so tedious.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.510
- It may be hard, but that
doesn't mean it's impossible.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.970
Swap Club will send you a
refurbished pair of AirPods

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.770
and try to repair your old ones.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.900
The startup developed a semi-automated way

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.270
to fix a variety of AirPod issues.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:43.170
- If you do it yourself,

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.420
it's probably gonna take
you an hour (chuckles).

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.570
And the automation allows
us to really scale this.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.960
- According to Swap club,
there's just one small problem.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.190
- In terms of repairability,

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.170
we see them just getting
tighter and tighter

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.240
and we have to continue
to change our process

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.730
to accommodate that with each generation.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.100
- There's a ton of tech
packed into this tiny thing,

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.020
and Apple adds more each year.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.300
The new H2 chip adds longer battery life.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.210
There's also a new skin detection sensor.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.070
And it has a new swipe interface

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.530
and improved microphones
for clearer calls.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:14.610
- In the same generation,

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.920
we've noticed some AirPods
have slight differences.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.740
The thing that we all love
the most about AirPods,

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:23.070
which is their tight,
small, compact design,

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.980
is a thing that actually
makes it most difficult

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:27.870
for them to be repaired.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.300
- Apple is working within some guardrails.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.370
It can only increase the size so much

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:35.100
if you want earbuds that
fit snugly in your ears.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.540
And Apple doesn't wanna make products

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.780
that are too complicated or
look too difficult to use.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.610
- So it's not just the manufacturers.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.350
We can't just blame the manufacturers

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.430
for creating the device.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.340
Consumers are driving the demand for it.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.140
- So after about two hours of work,

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.140
I only managed to tear down one AirPod

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.810
and obviously that thing's
never gonna work again.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:58.560
To be clear, repairability
isn't just an Apple issue.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.230
All rechargeable batteries
will die eventually.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.850
And it's a pretty common practice

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.620
to hold things together with glue.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.090
But the thing is, not all
are as hard to repair.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:11.090
(light music fades)

